      {
        "id": "https://openalex.org/W7171491943",
        "doi": "10.1016/j.autcon.2026.107162",
        "title": "Few-shot concrete surface extraction for automated UAV-based building inspection using vision foundation models",
        "published": "2026-07-28",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.autcon.2026.107162",
        "authors": [
          "Seungbo Shim",
          "Yeongjin Kim",
          "Hyeongmo Park"
        ],
        "institutions": [
          "Korea Institute of Civil Engineering and Building Technology",
          "Incheon National University",
          "Incheon Medical Center"
        ],
        "journal": "Automation in Construction",
        "abstract": "Although unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)-based building inspection requires automated extraction of concrete regions for end-to-end structural damage detection, existing automated inspection workflows lack this preprocessing step. This paper presents a few-shot learning pipeline that reformulates pixel-wise segmentation as a mask-level binary classification task, enabling site-adaptive concrete-region extraction with minimal annotation and no site-specific retraining. The pipeline pairs a frozen segmentation foundation model for mask generation with a frozen self-supervised vision transformer for mask-pooled features, training only a lightweight residual multilayer perceptron classifier. On two UAV datasets, it matches or exceeds supervised baselines trained under the same annotation budget, reaching 86.3% mean intersection over union on a complex multi-material dataset with ten labeled images. In real UAV crack inspection, restricting detection to the extracted concrete regions removes false alarms on non-concrete structures, increasing the crack F1 score from 3.5% to 27.9% and demonstrating direct field benefit for automated inspection.",
        "citation_count": 0,
        "method": "Hybrid"
      },
